1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,142
Collin:

2
00:00:00,142 --> 00:00:02,250
I haven’t seen you for a bit, Marie.

3
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:02,775
Marie:

4
00:00:02,775 --> 00:00:02,856
No.

5
00:00:02,856 --> 00:00:05,300
I’ve been busy with my project.

6
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:05,350
Collin:

7
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:09,513
You’re making a vegan alternative to eggs, aren’t you?

8
00:00:09,513 --> 00:00:11,970
Something that doesn’t use animal products?

9
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:12,359
Marie:

10
00:00:12,359 --> 00:00:12,748
Yes.

11
00:00:12,748 --> 00:00:14,304
I’m using chickpeas.

12
00:00:14,304 --> 00:00:19,751
I had two main aims when I first started looking for an alternative to

13
00:00:19,751 --> 00:00:20,140
eggs,

14
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:24,030
but actually I’ve found chickpeas have got more advantages.

15
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:24,905
Collin:

16
00:00:24,905 --> 00:00:25,780
Right.

17
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:26,187
Marie:

18
00:00:26,187 --> 00:00:31,473
But how about your project on reusing waste food – you were looking at

19
00:00:31,473 --> 00:00:33,100
bread, weren’t you?

20
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:33,522
Collin:

21
00:00:33,522 --> 00:00:33,945
Yes.

22
00:00:33,945 --> 00:00:36,057
It’s been hard work,

23
00:00:36,057 --> 00:00:38,169
but I’ve enjoyed it.

24
00:00:38,169 --> 00:00:43,660
The basic process was quite straightforward – breaking the stale bread down to a

25
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:45,350
paste then reforming it.

26
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:45,662
Marie:

27
00:00:45,662 --> 00:00:50,028
But you were using 3-D printing, weren’t you, to make the paste into

28
00:00:50,028 --> 00:00:50,340
biscuits?

29
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:50,701
Collin:

30
00:00:50,701 --> 00:00:52,868
Yeah, I’d used that before,

31
00:00:52,868 --> 00:00:58,648
but in this project, I had time to play around with different patterns for the biscuits

32
00:00:58,648 --> 00:01:03,704
and finding how I could add fruit and vegetables to make them a more

33
00:01:03,704 --> 00:01:04,427
appetising colour,

34
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:08,400
and I was really pleased with what I managed to produce.

35
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:08,738
Marie:

36
00:01:08,738 --> 00:01:13,808
It must’ve been a great feeling to make something appetising out of bits of

37
00:01:13,808 --> 00:01:16,850
old bread that would’ve been thrown away otherwise.

38
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,183
Collin:

39
00:01:17,183 --> 00:01:17,849
It was.

40
00:01:17,849 --> 00:01:22,841
And I’m hoping that some of the restaurants in town will be interested in

41
00:01:22,841 --> 00:01:23,507
the biscuits.

42
00:01:23,507 --> 00:01:26,170
I’m going to send them some samples.

43
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:26,576
Marie:

44
00:01:26,576 --> 00:01:31,448
I came across something on the internet yesterday that might interest you.

45
00:01:31,448 --> 00:01:36,320
It was a company that’s developed touch-sensitive sensors for food labels.

46
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:36,865
Collin:

47
00:01:36,865 --> 00:01:37,410
Mmm?

48
00:01:37,410 --> 00:01:37,764
Marie:

49
00:01:37,764 --> 00:01:41,661
It’s a special sort of label on the food package.

50
00:01:41,661 --> 00:01:46,974
When the label’s smooth, the food is fresh and then when you can feel

51
00:01:46,974 --> 00:01:48,391
bumps on the label,

52
00:01:48,391 --> 00:01:50,870
that means the food’s gone bad.

53
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:51,217
Marie:

54
00:01:51,217 --> 00:01:57,457
It started off as a project to help visually impaired people know whether food was fit to eat

55
00:01:57,457 --> 00:01:58,150
or not.

56
00:01:58,150 --> 00:01:58,672
Collin:

57
00:01:58,672 --> 00:01:59,193
Interesting.

58
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:01,280
So just solid food?

59
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:01,698
Marie:

60
00:02:01,698 --> 00:02:05,046
No, things like milk and juice as well.

61
00:02:05,046 --> 00:02:10,903
But actually, I thought it might be really good for drug storage in hospitals

62
00:02:10,903 --> 00:02:11,740
and pharmacies.

63
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:12,135
Collin:

64
00:02:12,135 --> 00:02:12,531
Right.

65
00:02:12,531 --> 00:02:18,463
And coming back to food, maybe it’d be possible to use it for other

66
00:02:18,463 --> 00:02:19,650
things besides freshness.

67
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:24,000
Like how many kilograms a joint of meat is, for example.

68
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:24,581
Marie:

69
00:02:24,581 --> 00:02:28,650
Yes, there’s all sorts of possibilities.

70
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:29,287
Collin:

71
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:38,205
I was reading an article about food trends predicting how eating habits might change

72
00:02:38,205 --> 00:02:41,390
in the next few years.

73
00:02:41,390 --> 00:02:41,584
Marie:

74
00:02:41,584 --> 00:02:43,137
Oh – things like more focus on local products?

75
00:02:43,137 --> 00:02:43,913
That seems so obvious,

76
00:02:43,913 --> 00:02:45,660
but the shops are still full of imported foods.

77
00:02:45,660 --> 00:02:45,992
Collin:

78
00:02:45,992 --> 00:02:49,310
Yes, they need to be more proactive to address that.

79
00:02:49,310 --> 00:02:49,811
Marie:

80
00:02:49,811 --> 00:02:53,320
And somehow motivate consumers to change, yes.

81
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:53,718
Collin:

82
00:02:53,718 --> 00:02:59,681
One thing everyone’s aware of is the need for a reduction in unnecessary packaging –

83
00:02:59,681 --> 00:03:04,849
but just about everything you buy in supermarkets is still covered in plastic.

84
00:03:04,849 --> 00:03:08,030
The government needs to do something about it.

85
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:08,543
Marie:

86
00:03:08,543 --> 00:03:09,056
Absolutely.

87
00:03:09,056 --> 00:03:11,620
It’s got to change.

88
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:12,022
Collin:

89
00:03:12,022 --> 00:03:17,250
Do you think there’ll be more interest in gluten- and lactose-free food?

90
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:17,631
Marie:

91
00:03:17,631 --> 00:03:20,302
For people with allergies or food intolerances?

92
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:21,828
I don’t know.

93
00:03:21,828 --> 00:03:27,169
Lots of people I know have been buying that type of food for years

94
00:03:27,169 --> 00:03:27,550
now.

95
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:27,861
Collin:

96
00:03:27,861 --> 00:03:31,280
Yes, even if they haven’t been diagnosed with an allergy.

97
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:31,703
Marie:

98
00:03:31,703 --> 00:03:32,973
That’s right.

99
00:03:32,973 --> 00:03:39,320
One thing I’ve noticed is the number of branded products related to celebrity chefs

100
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:44,821
– people watch them cooking on TV and then buy things like spice mixes

101
00:03:44,821 --> 00:03:48,630
or frozen foods with the chef’s name on .

102
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:48,680
.

103
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:48,730
.

104
00:03:48,730 --> 00:03:48,925
Marie:

105
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:50,695
I bought something like that once,

106
00:03:50,695 --> 00:03:52,170
but I won’t again.

107
00:03:52,170 --> 00:03:52,518
Collin:

108
00:03:52,518 --> 00:03:57,039
Yeah – I bought a ready-made spice mix for chicken which was supposed to

109
00:03:57,039 --> 00:04:00,518
be used by a chef I’d seen on television,

110
00:04:00,518 --> 00:04:03,300
and it didn’t actually taste of anything.

111
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:03,810
Marie:

112
00:04:03,810 --> 00:04:04,320
Mm.

113
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:09,930
Did the article mention ‘ghost kitchens’ used to produce takeaway food?

114
00:04:09,930 --> 00:04:10,306
Collin:

115
00:04:10,306 --> 00:04:10,682
No.

116
00:04:10,682 --> 00:04:11,810
What are they?

117
00:04:11,810 --> 00:04:12,168
Marie:

118
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:15,389
Well, they might have the name of a restaurant,

119
00:04:15,389 --> 00:04:20,756
but actually they’re a cooking facility just for delivery meals – the public don’t

120
00:04:20,756 --> 00:04:21,830
ever go there.

121
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:22,130
Marie:

122
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:26,030
But people aren’t aware of that – it’s all kept very quiet.

123
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:26,368
Collin:

124
00:04:26,368 --> 00:04:30,760
So people don’t realise the food’s not actually from the restaurant?

125
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:31,495
Marie:

126
00:04:31,495 --> 00:04:32,230
Right.

127
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:32,669
Collin:

128
00:04:32,669 --> 00:04:38,818
Did you know more and more people are using all sorts of different mushrooms

129
00:04:38,818 --> 00:04:41,453
now, to treat different health concerns?

130
00:04:41,453 --> 00:04:43,210
Things like heart problems?

131
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:43,607
Marie:

132
00:04:43,607 --> 00:04:44,003
Hmm.

133
00:04:44,003 --> 00:04:46,780
They might betaking a big risk there.

134
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:47,216
Collin:

135
00:04:47,216 --> 00:04:52,443
Yes, it’s hard to know which varieties are safe to eat.

136
00:04:52,443 --> 00:04:53,750
Anyway maybe now .

137
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:53,800
.

138
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:53,850
.
